{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhyML7ok9HenudzBycjD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate: **`sequential_pattern_alarm_survival`**. It passes the admission self-check, but on this data it fits clearly worse than the best model and its fit quality estimate is unreliable.\n\n**Hypothesis.** People read a sequence one flip at a time. At each flip a \"this is a pattern\" alarm may go off, and it gets more likely the longer the current predictable stretch has lasted. That stretch is either a run of identical flips or a chain of strict alternation. Alternation has to go on longer before it sets off the alarm, because people expect a fair coin to switch often. A sequence looks random to the extent it can be read to the end without the alarm firing. People also differ in how decisively this drives their choice.\n\nThis is the process-level account the brief asked for, and it aims at two of the critique's findings that survive the multiple-testing correction:\n- **Long streaks:** one long run condemns a sequence even when its overall switch count is ordinary.\n- **Perfect alternation:** long alternation is penalised only weakly.\n\n**How it's built.** For each distinct sequence, the model counts how often each stretch length (1 to 8 flips) occurs. The model is vectorised over trials, with two thresholds, one steepness parameter, and a person-level decisiveness term (a 400-slot population distribution, so the next design can predict new participants).\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Self-check:** prints `OK`. It loads as a module-level model, the log-probability is finite and the data contract holds.\n- **Full fit (4 chains × 1000 draws):** 0 divergent transitions, R-hat 1.00 and bulk ESS of at least 1150 on every parameter, so it should pass the convergence gate. I didn't time the run against the 30-minute limit.\n- **Fitted values:** the streak alarm's threshold is about 1.3 flips and the alternation alarm's about 6.1, so alternation triggers the alarm much later, as the hypothesis claims.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **Fit:** ELPD-LOO is −1052.8 against the best model's −995.1, about 58 nats behind. Pruning will most likely remove it unless it adds something on the next experiment's stimuli.\n- **Unreliable estimate:** 3.6% of trials have a high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance. The smoke fit gave the same warning, so its ELPD-LOO will probably be marked unreliable. The person-level decisiveness spread is large (about 1.34 on the log scale), which may be the cause, but I didn't confirm that.\n\nI also tried a variant that compares the two survival probabilities directly instead of their logs. It did worse (−1070.6) with even more high-Pareto-k trials, so I kept the log version.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_2`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1235,"cache_read_input_tokens":70892,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1235,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12bb4db1-211c-4336-8f92-c37a12c7d3db","uuid":"8e9bfc16-355d-49f6-9481-7153f18ef34a","timestamp":"2026-10-04T22:18:24.330Z","request_id":"req_011CfhyMKNu9rEYZhCsq4ERj"}
